= Ginger (Fresh and Dried)

_A single drug with two_ classically distinct drug-forms _that all three of Ayurveda, classical Chinese medicine, and Greco-Roman / medieval European pharmacy treat as separate entries: the fresh rhizome (ārdraka / 生薑 / zingiberis recens) and the dried rhizome (śuṇṭhī / 乾薑 / zingiberis aridum). The fresh-vs-dried distinction is the canonical cross-system convergence on substance-by-preparation differentiation — and modern pharmacology vindicates it precisely (gingerol →_ shogaol _on drying / heating). Both forms are fully canonical in all three Eurasian traditions; both are treated below in parallel._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Zingiber officinale_ Roscoe, family Zingiberaceae; the drug is the *rhizome*. _Zingiber_ comes from Greek ζιγγίβερις / ζιγγίβερι (_zingíberis / zingíberi_), itself a borrowing — via Hellenistic and Pali / Middle-Indic intermediaries — from the same South Indian / Dravidian source word that gave Pali _siṅgivera_ and Sanskrit शृङ्गवेर (_śṛṅgavera_, "horn-shaped"; from _śṛṅga_ "horn" + _vera_ "body"), referring to the antler-shape of the rhizome. So Greek _zingíberis_, Latin _zingiber_, English _ginger_, and Sanskrit _śṛṅgavera_ are the same word, with the Mediterranean form coming via maritime trade from south India to Alexandria. _officinale_ = "of the officina / pharmacy storeroom," the post-medieval Linnaean tag for officinal drugs.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _ζιγγίβερις_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. _ζιγγίβερις_, a clear Dravidian-via-Indic loan); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _zingiber_); Monier-Williams (s.v. _śṛṅgavera_); the south-Indian / Pali transmission chain is standard — see Mayrhofer, _Etymologisches Wörterbuch des Altindoarischen_, and the Tamil _iñci-vēr_ ("ginger-root") etymological connection.]

*English:* _Ginger_, from Old English _gingifer_, _gingiber_, ultimately from Late Latin _gingiber_, from Latin _zingiber_, from Greek as above. The English form preserves the soft-_g_ pronunciation through medieval French intermediaries.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "ginger, n.¹"); the Anglo-Norman / Old French intermediary is standard.]

*Chinese — the canonical two-form distinction:*
- *生薑 (shēngjiāng)*, "fresh / live ginger" — the fresh rhizome.
- *乾薑 (gānjiāng)*, "dried ginger" — the *peeled, dried* rhizome.
- *炮薑 (páojiāng)*, "blast-fried ginger" — the dried rhizome charred in a hot iron wok; a separately-treated, more warming, blood-staunching drug-form.
- *煨薑 (wēijiāng)*, "wrapped-roasted ginger" — fresh ginger roasted in wet paper or ashes; a middle position between fresh and dried, classically.
- *薑皮 (jiāngpí)*, "ginger peel" — the rind of fresh ginger, treated as a distinct drug for edema and surface disease.
The 麥 character is the wheat graph; 薑 (jiāng) is the ginger graph, a phono-semantic compound with the grass radical 艸 / 艹. The 生薑 / 乾薑 distinction is one of the foundational drug-form differentiations of Chinese pharmacy and goes back to the _Shennong Bencao Jing_.#footnote[Character glosses and drug-form distinctions: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); 生薑 and 乾薑 as separately-treated drugs from the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經) onward; full treatment in _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, vegetable section; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004), which treats 生薑, 乾薑, and 炮薑 in separate entries.]

*Sanskrit — the parallel two-form distinction:*
- *आर्द्रक (ārdraka)*, "the moist one" — *fresh* ginger; from _ārdra_ "wet, moist."
- *शुण्ठी (śuṇṭhī)* — *dried* ginger; from _śuṣ-_ "to dry" via Prakritic intermediaries.
- *नागर (nāgara)*, "the urbane / civilized one" — a common synonym for dried ginger, with the connotation of refinement.
- *विश्वा (viśvā)*, "the universal one" — synonym for dried ginger, capturing its near-universal applicability.
- *महौषध (mahauṣadha)*, "the great medicine" — honorific for dried ginger.
- *कटुभद्र (kaṭubhadra)*, "the auspicious pungent" — synonym.
- *शृङ्गवेर (śṛṅgavera)*, "horn-bodied" — the Sanskrit cognate of Greek _zingíberis_ (above); generic for ginger, sometimes specifically the fresh rhizome.
The ārdraka / śuṇṭhī distinction is the foundational Ayurvedic drug-form differentiation, classed since the saṃhitās as effectively two drugs of overlapping but distinct character — ārdraka cooler and lighter, śuṇṭhī hotter and heavier; the dried form is one of the three drugs of _trikaṭu_ ("the three pungents": _śuṇṭhī_, _pippalī_, _marica_).#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _ārdraka_, _śuṇṭhī_, _nāgara_, _viśvā_, _mahauṣadha_, _śṛṅgavera_); the ārdraka / śuṇṭhī distinction is canonical from Caraka Saṃhitā onward; the _trikaṭu_ formula and śuṇṭhī's place in it: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

The 生薑 / 乾薑 distinction is *the* canonical example of a single drug treated by Chinese pharmacy as functionally two distinct drugs by preparation. Both are in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (生薑 in the middle class, 乾薑 in the middle class).#footnote[Both 生薑 and 乾薑 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經); full treatment in _Bencao Gangmu_, 1578; systematized in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., with separate entries.]

*生薑 (fresh ginger):*
- Property: warm (溫); Flavor: pungent (辛); Channels: Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).
- Actions: 發散風寒 — releases external wind-cold (the classical use for common cold with wind-cold pattern); 溫中止嘔 — warms the middle and stops vomiting (the canonical antiemetic — for cold-pattern nausea, morning sickness, motion sickness, the canonical "physicians' ginger" 嘔家聖藥); 化痰止咳 — transforms phlegm and stops cough; 解魚蟹毒 / 解半夏天南星毒 — *detoxifies fish/crab poisoning and the toxicity of pinellia and arisaema* (the classical ginger-pinellia pairing for safety).#footnote[生薑 actions: _Bencao Gangmu_ 生薑 entry; "嘔家聖藥" (the holy medicine of the vomiting patient) is the classical epithet; the pinellia/arisaema detoxification role is canonical and is the basis of formula-safety pairing in many classical prescriptions. Cf. Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*乾薑 (dried ginger):*
- Property: *hot* (熱); Flavor: pungent (辛); Channels: Heart (心), Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Kidney (腎) — *additionally to the fresh-ginger channels, the Heart and Kidney*. The expanded channel scope (in particular Heart and Kidney) is the classical mark of the deeper warming reach of dried ginger.
- Actions: 溫中散寒 — warms the middle and disperses cold (chief use for vacuity-cold of Spleen-Stomach, epigastric cold pain, watery vomit, watery diarrhea); 回陽通脈 — *restores yang and unblocks the vessels* — the central indication of 乾薑 in 四逆湯 and the cold-collapse formulas; 溫肺化飲 — warms the Lung and transforms thin phlegm (cold-pattern phlegm-fluid cough, thin watery sputum); 溫經止血 — warms the channels and stops bleeding (the basis of 炮薑 charred-ginger use in cold-pattern uterine bleeding).#footnote[乾薑 actions: _Bencao Gangmu_ 乾薑 entry; 四逆湯's central use of 乾薑 + 附子 + 炙甘草 is the canonical cold-collapse therapy; cf. Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*炮薑 (blast-fried / charred ginger):*
- Property: hot; Flavor: bitter, astringent, pungent. The charring intensifies the warming and adds astringency. Principal use: chronic cold-pattern diarrhea with vacuity, cold-pattern uterine bleeding (the classical use in 黃土湯), persistent cold abdominal pain.#footnote[炮薑 entry: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; 黃土湯 from _Jīnguì Yàolüè_.]

*Comparative summary (Chinese reading):* fresh ginger is *outward-going* — releases exterior, stops surface vomit, detoxifies acute toxins — while dried ginger is *inward-going* — restores yang in the deep collapse, warms the chronic vacuity-cold interior. *The same plant, by drying alone, acquires a different therapeutic territory and an extended channel-scope. This is the Chinese-pharmacy paradigm of_ saṃskāra _(preparation transforming substance), and it converges precisely with the Ayurvedic ārdraka / śuṇṭhī distinction.*

*Cautions for both forms:* yin-vacuity heat, bleeding from heat, hypertension with heat pattern; pregnancy (heavier doses).#footnote[Cautions: standard TCM; bleeding from heat-pattern is the classical absolute contraindication.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

The ārdraka / śuṇṭhī distinction is one of the foundational drug-form differentiations of the Ayurvedic canon.#footnote[Treatment of ārdraka and śuṇṭhī as parallel drug-forms: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_) and Cikitsāsthāna throughout; Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, _ārdraka_ and _śuṇṭhī_ entries (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_ (Chaukhamba).]

*आर्द्रक (ārdraka, fresh ginger):*
- *Rasa:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).
- *Guṇa:* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous — the fresh juiciness), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp).
- *Vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming).
- *Vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — the famous *exception* (a pungent-rasa drug with sweet vipāka, a counterintuitive transformation, attesting to fresh ginger's tonifying / nourishing depth despite the surface pungency).
- *Doṣa:* Pacifies Vāta and Kapha; mildly Pitta-aggravating in excess.

*शुण्ठी (śuṇṭhī, dried ginger):*
- *Rasa:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).
- *Guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light — the drying process *lightens* the rhizome), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, retained from the residual fixed oil), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, intensified).
- *Vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming — *stronger* than ārdraka).
- *Vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — the same characteristic counterintuitive vipāka.
- *Doṣa:* Pacifies Vāta and Kapha (more strongly than ārdraka); aggravates Pitta more readily.#footnote[Comparative rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka of ārdraka and śuṇṭhī: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; the *madhura vipāka of a pungent-rasa drug* is a famous and frequently-commented-on exception in the dravyaguna literature, and underwrites the classical use of both ginger forms as _agni-dīpana_ AND _bṛṃhaṇa_ (a contradiction unless vipāka is sweet). Cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II.]

*Prabhāva:* No distinct prabhāva is classically named for either form, but the *combination of pungent rasa with sweet vipāka* — a contradictory parameter-pairing — is itself sometimes read at prabhāva level by commentary; the *sleep-disturbing and Vāta-pacifying-yet-tīkṣṇa* profile is what gives ginger its near-universal _anupāna_ / _saṃskāra_ status. Flagged as commentarial/interpretive.

*Karma (actions) — common to both forms, with intensities and specifics varying:*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles the digestive fire, supports digestion of accumulated _āma_ (universal).
- _Āma-pācana_ — burns up undigested metabolic material; the key "detox" action in Ayurvedic practice.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — promotes downward / forward movement of Vāta (relieves bloating, intestinal gas, constipation of Vāta type).
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic.
- _Vṛṣya_ (modest, especially śuṇṭhī).
- _Kāsa-hara_, _śvāsa-hara_ — relieves cough and dyspnea (cold-pattern).
- _Pratiśyāya_, _jvara_ (early stages) — for common cold and incipient fever (the famous classical "ginger tea at the first sign of cold").
- _Āma-vāta_ (a form of joint disease with metabolic toxin involvement) — śuṇṭhī is a chief drug.
- _Sandhi-vāta_ (osteoarthritis, joint Vāta) — śuṇṭhī.
- _Antiemetic_ — both forms, ārdraka especially for acute / surface vomit (parallel to 生薑 嘔家聖藥); śuṇṭhī for chronic vacuity-cold vomit (parallel to 乾薑 溫中).

*Ārdraka-specific:* fresh juice (_ārdraka-svarasa_) as a first-line antiemetic; preferred for acute disease, surface symptoms, surface phlegm-cough. _Lavaṇārdraka_ (salted fresh ginger before meals) is a classical _agni-dīpana_ aperitif.

*Śuṇṭhī-specific:* central drug in _trikaṭu_ (with _pippalī_ Piper longum and _marica_ Piper nigrum) — the foundational "three pungents" formula for Kapha-clearing, _agni-dīpana_, and _āma-pācana_. Principal drug in chronic vacuity-cold abdominal disorders, in _āmavāta_ and _sandhi-vāta_, and in chronic respiratory disorders.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27, and Cikitsāsthāna throughout (esp. _grahaṇī_, _āmavāta_, _vātavyādhi_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; standard Ayurvedic clinical use. _Trikaṭu_: standard formula across the saṃhitās and nighaṇṭus. The ārdraka-svarasa antiemetic and lavaṇārdraka uses: classical dietetics and Caraka.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:* contraindicated in Pitta excess, _rakta-pitta_ (bleeding from heat), _amlapitta_ (hyperacidity with heat), high fever with Pitta, summer heat — the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile inflames Pitta states; *śuṇṭhī aggravates Pitta more than ārdraka*.
- *(F) Classical Chinese folk caveat:* "fresh ginger in autumn / nights" — a classical Chinese folk warning attributed to Confucius and to Sun Simiao, that fresh ginger eaten in autumn evenings disturbs sleep and qi; this is folk-Chinese, not Ayurvedic.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Antiplatelet effect:* gingerols and shogaols are mild platelet inhibitors; high-dose ginger (>3–4 g/day dried, or large daily juiced amounts) co-administered with anticoagulants (warfarin, NOACs) or antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel) can theoretically increase bleeding risk. Documented case reports of INR elevation with ginger + warfarin. *Use cautiously in concurrent anticoagulant therapy.*
  - *Gallstones:* relative caution — ginger increases bile flow; in symptomatic cholelithiasis, may precipitate biliary colic; ask patients with gallstones to use cautiously.
  - *Pregnancy:* paradoxically, ginger is *one of the best-studied antiemetics for morning sickness* (multiple RCTs, modest effect; ACOG endorses it). The classical caution about pregnancy doses comes from very high doses (>2 g/day dried) at full term, where the antiplatelet effect could conceivably matter; at typical antiemetic doses (0.5–1.5 g/day), the safety record is excellent.
  - *Cardiovascular medication:* additive antihypertensive effect at high doses.
  - *GERD:* high doses may worsen reflux in heat-pattern reflux.
  - *Diabetes / hypoglycemia:* mild hypoglycemic effect; monitor diabetics on tight glycemic control.#footnote[Antiplatelet / anticoagulant interaction: documented in herb-drug-interaction literature (cf. _Herb Contraindications and Drug Interactions_, Mills & Bone; _Drugs in Pregnancy and Lactation_, Briggs). Pregnancy ginger antiemetic evidence: well-established; ACOG and Cochrane reviews endorse modest effect for morning sickness. Other (M) cautions are standard herb-drug references — verify against current systematic reviews.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Ginger is canonical in Tibb and is one of the few spices with explicit Qur'ānic attestation. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* زنجبيل (_zanjabīl_) — the canonical name; the same word in Persian (زنجبیل _zanjabīl_) and Urdu (ادرک _adrak_ for fresh, زنجبیل _zanjabīl_ for dried; سونٹھ _sonṭh_ is the Hindi-Urdu word for dried). The Arabic word _zanjabīl_ is itself a Sanskrit-via-Pali loan, from _śṛṅgavera_ via Persian intermediaries — same etymological family as Greek ζιγγίβερις and English "ginger."

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* *Qur'an 76:17* (Sūrat al-Insān): _"And they will be given to drink there a cup mixed with zanjabīl"_ — ginger as one of the drinks of paradise; the verse is the foundational Qur'ānic attestation of ginger as a blessed substance. Hadith references in the Prophetic-medicine compendia.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 3rd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _zanjabīl_; Ibn al-Bayṭār). The high-degree Hot-Dry classification — *the canonical warming-and-drying drug of Tibb* — converges with the Ayurvedic _uṣṇa vīrya_ and with the Chinese 溫 / 熱 classification (especially for 乾薑 dried ginger). One of the cleanest tri-system convergences.

*Af'āl:* *Muḥallil* (resolvent); *muḥarrik* (stimulant); *muqawwī al-mi'da* (gastric tonic); *mufariḥ al-qalb* (mood-elevating); *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac); *munaffith* (expectorant); *mughayyir li-l-balgham* (phlegm-modifying); *muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain tonic in moderate dose); *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent).

*Istemālāt:* phlegmatic and cold diseases (the principal humoral indication); cold-pattern dyspepsia; chronic cough with thin phlegm; impotence; arthritis (cold-pattern); intestinal worms; nausea and vomit; cold-pattern paralysis.

*Mawāne':* bilious / hot-pattern conditions; hemorrhoids with bleeding; pregnancy at high dose; hot-pattern fever; gastric ulcer.

*Muṣliḥ:* sugar (*sukkar*); rose-water; almond oil; for severe heating, milk and ghee.

*Badal:* black pepper (*filfil aswad*) — both are pungent-warming class.

*Tibb formulas:* *Maʿjūn-i Zanjabīl* (ginger electuary) — for digestive and cold-pattern conditions; *Sharbat-i Zanjabīl* — ginger syrup for cough and gastric weakness; *Ḥabb-i Zanjabīl* (ginger pills) — for cold-pattern impotence; *Sufūf-i Sonṭh* (dried ginger powder) — for indigestion. *Joshānda-yi Zanjabīl* — decoction for fever recovery and gastric weakness.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _zanjabīl_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; al-Rāzī, *al-Ḥāwī*; Anṭākī, *Tadhkirah*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _adrak / sonṭh / zanjabīl_ entries; Ibn Qayyim, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_.#footnote[Tibb zanjabīl: Qur'an 76:17 establishes the Prophetic-medicine status; classical Tibb pharmacology in Avicenna and Ibn al-Bayṭār; the tri-system convergence with Ayurvedic and Chinese readings is one of the cleanest in the materia medica.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

A short list — ginger appears in a substantial fraction of all classical Chinese formulas; representative examples:

*With 生薑 (fresh ginger):*
- *桂枝湯 (Guìzhī Tāng, "Cinnamon Twig Decoction")* — the foundational exterior-harmonizing formula; 生薑 + 桂枝 + 白芍 + 大棗 + 炙甘草; for wind-cold with deficiency.
- *小青龍湯 (Xiǎo Qīng Lóng Tāng, "Minor Bluegreen Dragon Decoction")* — for wind-cold with thin phlegm; 生薑 + 麻黃 + 桂枝 + 半夏 + 細辛 + 五味子 + 乾薑 (note both fresh and dried!) + 白芍 + 炙甘草. A formula combining both forms.
- *半夏厚朴湯 (Bànxià Hòupǔ Tāng, "Pinellia and Magnolia Bark Decoction")* — the canonical 梅核氣 (plum-pit qi) formula; 生薑 as both action ingredient and pinellia-detoxifier.
- *Many countless others* — fresh ginger is one of the most-used "envoy" herbs in the Chinese pharmacy.

*With 乾薑 (dried ginger):*
- *四逆湯 (Sìnì Tāng, "Frigid Extremities Decoction")* — the canonical cold-collapse formula; 乾薑 + 附子 + 炙甘草. Restoration of yang in the deep cold; 乾薑 supports 附子's central warming and protects against its toxicity.
- *理中湯 (Lǐ Zhōng Tāng, "Regulate the Middle Decoction")* — 乾薑 + 人參 + 白朮 + 炙甘草; for cold-vacuity of Spleen-Stomach with watery diarrhea, cold abdominal pain.
- *甘草乾薑湯 (Gāncǎo Gānjiāng Tāng, "Licorice and Dried Ginger Decoction")* — two-herb formula for upper-burner cold with thin saliva.

*With 炮薑 (charred ginger):*
- *黃土湯 (Huáng Tǔ Tāng, "Yellow Earth Decoction")* — for cold-vacuity uterine bleeding / hematemesis; 炮薑 + 灶心黃土 (cooking-pot yellow earth) + 阿膠 + 黃芩 + 地黃 + 白朮 + 附子 + 甘草.#footnote[Formula sources: Zhang Zhongjing, _Shanghan Lun_ (傷寒論) and _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ (金匱要略); compositions: Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009). The pervasiveness of ginger in classical formulas is itself a comment on the centrality of the drug.]

=== Ayurveda

*With ārdraka (fresh ginger):*
- *Ārdraka-svarasa* — the fresh juice, ~5–10 mL, taken plain or with honey, the classical antiemetic and _agni-dīpana_.
- *Lavaṇārdraka* — fresh ginger sliced and salted, taken before meals as an _agni-dīpana_ aperitif (the Ayurvedic _amuse-bouche_).
- *Mahālakṣmīvilāsa rasa, Tribhuvana-kīrti rasa* — classical compound formulas with ginger juice as _bhāvanā_ (liquid trituration medium).

*With śuṇṭhī (dried ginger):*
- *Trikaṭu (त्रिकटु)* — the foundational "three pungents" formula: equal parts śuṇṭhī, _pippalī_ (Piper longum), and _marica_ (Piper nigrum), in powder form, ~0.5–2 g taken with honey before meals. The classical _agni-dīpana_, _kapha-hara_, _āma-pācana_ formula; cited everywhere in the saṃhitās.
- *Triphalā guggulu, Yogarāj guggulu, Kaiśora guggulu* — many of the major guggulu compound formulas include śuṇṭhī.
- *Śuṇṭhī kvātha* — dried ginger decoction, for acute cold-pattern fever and respiratory disease.
- *Mahānārāyaṇa taila, Bālā-aśvagandhādi taila* — śuṇṭhī as ingredient in Vāta-pacifying medicated oils.
- *Dadhi-mastu + śuṇṭhī* — buttermilk with dried ginger; classical preparation for _grahaṇī_ (malabsorption / IBS-like syndrome).
- *Sauṇṭhādi kvātha* — śuṇṭhī-led decoction for chronic respiratory disease.
- *Pippalī-rasāyana, Saubhāgya-śuṇṭhī-pāka* — śuṇṭhī in compound _rasāyana_ contexts.#footnote[Formula sources: standard Ayurvedic formularies — _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_, _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_, _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I; Caraka Saṃhitā Cikitsāsthāna throughout. _Trikaṭu_: ubiquitous classical formula. Saubhāgya-śuṇṭhī-pāka: postpartum tonic from regional Ayurvedic tradition.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

Ginger is one of the most-documented Greco-Roman spice-drugs.

- *Greco-Roman:* _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_ II.160, _zingíberis_) describes ginger as a hot, digestion-aiding root, with the Indian / Arabian origin noted; used for digestion, gas, stomach pain, "phlegmatic" complaints. _Pliny_ (_Naturalis Historia_ XII) places it among the major Eastern spice imports. _Galen_ (_On the Properties of Foodstuffs_) classes ginger as warming and moistening in the second degree, with digestive and warming uses.
- *Roman / medieval Mediterranean:* ginger was one of the four canonical "hot spices" of medieval European medical and culinary practice (with pepper, cinnamon, and grains of paradise); the medieval _Antidotarium Nicolai_ and _Salernitan_ corpus give numerous ginger-containing preparations.
- *Hildegard von Bingen* (_Physica_, late 12th c.) treats Ingwer with characteristic combination of caution (warming, drying; not for the healthy lest it inflame) and use (for the sick weakened by cold).
- *Classical galenic preparations:* _Tinctura zingiberis_, _Syrupus zingiberis_, _Confectio zingiberis_, _Trochisci zingiberis_; the British Pharmaceutical Codex carried these into the 20th c. Crystallized / candied ginger (German _Ingwer in Sirup_, English candied ginger) is the modern descendant of the medieval sugared-spice tradition.
- *Modern Western:* ginger persists in the modern pharmacopoeia as a digestive / antiemetic, particularly for motion sickness and pregnancy nausea (clinical evidence supports this). Ginger ale and ginger beer descend from the medieval / early-modern fermented-ginger tradition.#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ II.160 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XII (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_ (Powell translation). Medieval and Salernitan tradition: standard pharmacy histories (e.g. Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_; the _Antidotarium Nicolai_). Hildegard: _Physica_ (Throop translation). Galenic preparations: pharmacopoeial history.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (and the gingerol-→-shogaol story):*
- *Gingerols* — the principal pungent compounds of *fresh* ginger; *6-gingerol* is the dominant member.
- *Shogaols* — formed by dehydration of gingerols on *drying / heating*; *6-shogaol* is the dominant member of dried ginger; *more pungent and more thermogenic* than gingerols.
- *Zingerone* — formed by retro-aldol cleavage of gingerols on higher heat / long cooking; less pungent, more aromatic.
- *Paradols* — from further reduction.
- *Essential oil:* zingiberene, β-bisabolene, α-curcumene, α-farnesene, β-sesquiphellandrene; the aromatic top-note of both fresh and dried ginger.
- *Starch* — substantial in the rhizome; fixed oil ~3–5%.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Phytochemistry_ and _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_; the gingerol→shogaol→zingerone heat-transformation pathway is well characterized and is the molecular basis of the fresh-vs-dried distinction.]

*The classical fresh-vs-dried distinction — biochemically vindicated:* the drying process transforms gingerols (cooler, surface-acting, less penetrating) into shogaols (hotter, more thermogenic, more penetrating), and further heat converts these to zingerone. The classical Chinese 生薑 → 乾薑 → 炮薑 sequence and the Ayurvedic ārdraka → śuṇṭhī sequence reflect *exactly* this chemistry: the dried/heated forms are *hotter*, with *deeper* (channel-extended in TCM; vipāka-amplified in Ayurveda) action. *This is one of the cleanest classical-modern convergences in the entire materia medica: a substance-form distinction that all three Eurasian classical traditions arrived at independently, and that modern pharmacology vindicates molecule-for-molecule.*

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antiemetic:* well-established; multiple RCTs and meta-analyses support modest-to-moderate efficacy in motion sickness, postoperative nausea, pregnancy morning sickness, and chemotherapy-induced nausea. ACOG endorses ginger for morning sickness; clinical evidence is among the strongest for any herb-derived antiemetic.
- *Anti-inflammatory:* gingerols and shogaols inhibit COX-1, COX-2, and 5-LOX (the lipoxygenase / leukotriene pathway); modest clinical evidence for osteoarthritis pain (small effect) and dysmenorrhea (small-moderate effect).
- *Antiplatelet:* mild but real; the classical-modern correlate of the warming / blood-moving / 活血 attribution; relevant for anticoagulant interaction.
- *Digestive / prokinetic:* increases gastric motility; modest improvement in functional dyspepsia and idiopathic gastroparesis.
- *Glycemic:* modest hypoglycemic effect in type-2 diabetes (small RCTs).
- *Cardiovascular:* mild blood-pressure lowering, lipid-improving effects.
- *Antimicrobial:* in vitro activity broad-spectrum; clinical relevance modest.
- *Thermogenic / metabolic:* shogaols (dried ginger) more thermogenic than gingerols — biochemical correlate of śuṇṭhī's deeper warming.#footnote[Clinical evidence: multiple Cochrane reviews and meta-analyses — verify against current systematic reviews in _BMJ_, _Cochrane Database_, _Journal of Pain_, _Obstetrics & Gynecology_. The antiemetic evidence is one of the strongest in the herbal pharmacopoeia. Antiplatelet effect: documented in pharmacology literature; clinically relevant in anticoagulant co-administration.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Fresh ginger (food):* no toxicological upper limit; routine culinary use raises no safety question.
- *Dried ginger / powder (medicinal):* commonly 0.5–3 g/day; clinical-trial ranges 0.5–2 g/day for nausea, up to 3–4 g/day for inflammation; doses >4 g/day approach the antiplatelet-relevant range.
- *Ginger essential oil:* not for oral use except under qualified supervision; concentrated and irritant.
- *Pregnancy (antiemetic use):* 0.5–1.5 g/day of dried ginger is well-studied and considered safe; high doses near term should be moderated given the antiplatelet effect.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously):* (a) concurrent *anticoagulant* / antiplatelet therapy (high doses); (b) symptomatic *gallstones*; (c) Pitta-pattern / heat-pattern conditions (Ayurvedic /TCM caution); (d) heavy GERD; (e) the third trimester at very high doses (theoretical bleeding-risk consideration); (f) acute Pitta-aggravation / bleeding-from-heat — absolute classical caution.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_, vol. 1, _Rhizoma Zingiberis_; Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India (śuṇṭhī monograph); Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (生薑 and 乾薑); current clinical-trial literature. These are reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.]

